+# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright contributors to the vLLM project
+"""CUDA-graph-capture-safe batched prepare/finalize (replicated-token EP).
+
+Drop-in replacement for the reference ``BatchedPrepareAndFinalize`` for the
+``BatchedExperts`` activation format. The reference dispatch is eager-only — it
+has a Python ``for expert_id in range(...)`` loop, an ``if rows == 0``
+device->host sync, and a boolean-mask gather with a data-dependent output shape
+— so it cannot be captured into a CUDA graph (it raises
+``cudaErrorStreamCaptureUnsupported``) and is O(num_experts) host-synced per
+layer, which is catastrophically slow for a real (128-expert) MoE.
+
+This class produces the same batched layout and combine, but with only
+capture-safe ops: no ``.item()`` / host branches / data-dependent shapes. The
+per-expert row capacity is ``min(max_num_tokens, num_tokens)`` rather than a
+flat ``max_num_tokens``: a batch of T tokens fills at most T rows of any
+expert slot, and T is CPU-visible and constant per captured graph — so decode
+graphs carry tiny ``[E, capture_size, K]`` buffers instead of
+``[E, max_num_batched_tokens, K]`` (orders of magnitude less graph memory and
+capacity-shaped work), while big eager prefill chunks still get full capacity.
+
+Key idea: a token routed to expert ``e`` lands at row ``dest`` = its 0-based rank
+among all routings to ``e``. The reference computes ``dest`` with a host-synced
+counter; here it is a **within-expert rank via stable argsort** (stable sort by
+expert id preserves flat order within each expert) — deterministic and
+fixed-shape. Because it is deterministic, ``finalize`` can recompute the exact
+same mapping from ``topk_ids`` (a graph input); in practice it reuses the plan
+``prepare`` computed via an identity-keyed cache (see ``_dispatch_plan_cached``;
+the plan is costly enough that computing it twice per layer matters), falling
+back to recomputation whenever the ``topk_ids`` object differs.
+
+EP model (mirrors the reference ``BatchedPrepareAndFinalize``): tokens are
+**replicated** across the EP group (pure EP, ``dp_size == 1`` — vLLM hands this
+P/F a non-batched ``no_dp_ep`` plan, which the oracle bridges to this one), and
+this rank owns the contiguous expert shard ``[first_expert, first_expert + E)``
+where ``first_expert = E * rank`` and ``E = num_local_experts``. ``_dispatch_plan``
+subtracts ``first_expert`` from the **global** ``topk_ids`` so each rank only
+gathers tokens routed to its local experts; routings to other ranks' experts
+fall out of range and are dropped. Each rank thus computes a partial output, and
+the runner all-reduces across the EP group (``output_is_reduced() == False``).
+
+Scope: deferred/unquantized input only (``expects_unquantized_inputs`` experts,
+e.g. the paired-4:8 NVFP4 backend, which quantize inside ``apply()``); asserts if
+asked to quantize in prepare. Assumes the default **contiguous** EP layout (rank
+``r`` owns globals ``[r*E, (r+1)*E)``); a non-contiguous ``expert_map`` (EPLB) or
+sequence-parallel token sharding are not yet handled.
+
+The EP>1 partial-sum invariant (per-rank partials all-reduce to the single-rank
+result) is covered by ``tests/kernels/moe/test_paired48_nvfp4_ep.py``.
